
  [1mThe fork (third question):[0m "persists" needs a concrete storage location, and this is the one choice that's genuinely
  expensive to reverse — once IDs are written to a particular store on real browsers, changing it means a migration path
  for users who already have the old value.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close and browser restart, readable by any code on the origin, one simple synchronous
    API. Matches "persist" and "other forms need it" directly. The cost: any XSS on the page can read it, so the ID can
    never double as an auth credential — it's a correlation key only.
  - [1mJS-readable cookie[0m — also persists, and gets sent to the server automatically on requests. Adds expiry and
    domain/path semantics you have to get right, ships on every request whether you want it or not, and is no more
    secure than localStorage if JS can read it.
  - [1m[38;5;153mhttpOnly[39m cookie, server-set[0m — the actually-secure option: JS can't read it, so XSS can't steal it. But that's
    precisely the problem here — you said other forms need the value, and they'd have no way to read it. It only works
    if the server, not the client, attaches identity to requests.

  My recommendation: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, with an explicit rule written into the spec that this ID is a tracking/correlation
  key and never an authentication token. If it ever needs to authenticate anything, it has to move to [38;5;153mhttpOnly[39m and the
  client stops reading it.

  I'll ask about the privacy/consent side and what "track" writes to next — those are the last two before I lay out
  approaches.
